<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Hutchinson (governor)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a>–<a href="/years/1780">1780</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Hutchinson (1711–1780) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> merchant, historian and politician who served as lieutenant governor and then governor of the Province of Massachusetts Bay from 1771 to 1774. A loyalist, he enforced British policy, and his insistence that the taxed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> be landed led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a> in 1773. He left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1774 and died there in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-hutchinson-governor.jpg" alt="Thomas Hutchinson (governor)" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Truman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ThomasHutchinsonByEdwardTruman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
